sentences.t:
Todos los días a mio Çid aguardavan
moros de las fronteras e unas yentes extrañas;
sanó el rey Fáriz, con él se consejavan.

sentences.l:
All the days my Çid they-were-watching
Moors of the frontiers and some peoples strange;
healed the king Fáriz, with him themselves they-were-counselling.

sentences.t:
Aguijó mio Çid, ivas cabadelant,
y ffincó en un poyo que es sobre Mont Real;
alto es el poyo, maravilloso e grant;
non teme guerra, sabet, a nulla part.

sentences.l:
Spurred my Çid, went-himself forward,
there stopped on a hill which is above Mont Real;
high is the hill, marvellous and great;
not fears war, know-ye, at no side.

sentences.t:
Mio Çid R[o]y Díaz de Dios aya su graçia!
Ido es a Castiella Álbar Fáñez Minaya,
treynta cavallos al rey los enpresentava;

sentences.l:
My Çid R[o]y Díaz from God may-he-have his grace!
Gone is to Castiella Álbar Fáñez Minaya,
thirty horses to-the king them he-was-presenting;

sentences.t:
A vos, rey ondrado, enbía esta presentaja;
bésavos los pie[de]s e las manos amas
quel ay[a]des merçed, si el Criador vos vala.”

sentences.l:
To you, king honoured, he-sends this present;
he-kisses-you the feet and the hands both
that-him you-may-have mercy, so the Creator you may-protect.”

sentences.t:
Besóle las manos Minaya Álbar Fáñez:
“Grado e graçias, rey, commo a señor natural;
esto feches agora, al feredes adelant;
con Dios nós guisaremos commo vós lo fagades.”

sentences.l:
Kissed-him the hands Minaya Álbar Fáñez:
“Thanks and thanks, king, as to lord natural;
this you-do now, the-rest you-will-do hereafter;
with God we will-contrive how you it may-do.”
